From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 18:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: do not free the task array twice

rocket_copy_tasks() allocates rjob->tasks and, on any failure inside its
copy loop, frees it again on the fail: path -- without clearing the
pointer.  Its only caller, rocket_ioctl_submit_job(), then unwinds
through rocket_job_put() -> rocket_job_cleanup(), which frees
job->tasks unconditionally.  The same allocation is handed to the
allocator twice.

Two failures reach it, and both are ordinary userspace input:

  copy_struct_from_user() fails      a bad task pointer, or a trailing
                                     field this kernel does not know
  task.regcmd_count == 0             a task that asks for no registers

The second is not hypothetical: a userspace whose register-program
generator REFUSES a shape leaves regcmd_count at its zeroed value, and a
caller that submits anyway lands exactly here.

/dev/accel/accel0 is group `render`, so this is an unprivileged double
free of a kmalloc-16 object -- one of the hottest caches in the kernel.
The freelist then hands one object to two owners, and the damage
surfaces far from here, in whatever allocates next.  Observed as random
faults in unrelated subsystems: a scatterlist walked in
drm_gem_shmem_release() from rocket_gem_bo_free(), and a wild
phys_to_virt() inside swiotlb_bounce() reached from
drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_sgt() -- neither of which is a defect in those
paths.

With slub_debug=FZPU it is deterministic: two "BUG kmalloc-16: Object
already free" reports per run of a client that submits rejected jobs,
naming rocket_ioctl_submit() as the allocation site and
rocket_job_cleanup() as the second free.

Fix it by giving the array a single owner.  rocket_job_cleanup() already
frees it on every path out of the ioctl, including the ones that never
reach the hardware, so the fail: path only has to stop.

This is mainline code that the RK3576 series does not introduce, and the
RK3588 reaches the same path.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index c4ad600d0..da4f4241b 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -229,7 +229,11 @@ rocket_copy_tasks(struct drm_device *dev,
 	return 0;
 
 fail:
-	kvfree(rjob->tasks);
+	/*
+	 * Do NOT free rjob->tasks here.  Every caller unwinds through
+	 * rocket_job_cleanup(), which frees it unconditionally, so freeing it
+	 * on this path hands the same object to the allocator twice.
+	 */
 	return ret;
 }
 
